---
import { GITHUB_URL, NPM_PACKAGE_URL, RELEASES_URL } from "../../shared/config/navigation.js";
import SiteShell from "../../app/providers/site-shell.astro";
import { installCommands } from "../../shared/config/install-commands.js";
import Callout from "../../shared/ui/callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../shared/ui/code-block.astro";
import CodeTabs from "../../shared/ui/code-tabs.astro";
import Heading from "../../shared/ui/heading.astro";
import PageHeader from "../../shared/ui/page-header.astro";
import TextLink from "../../shared/ui/text-link.astro";
---

<SiteShell
  title="Getting started"
  description="Install type-tune on Node.js 22+ and run the first TypeScript scan."
  withSidebar
>
  <article class="prose">
    <PageHeader
      eyebrow="Usage"
      title="Getting started"
      lead="Install the published CLI, confirm your platform, and scan one TypeScript project."
    />

    <section class="content-section">
      <Heading>What you need</Heading>
      <p>
        The npm package requires <strong>Node.js 22 or newer</strong>. Its launcher runs a
        platform-specific Rust binary. Supported hosts are:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>macOS arm64 and x64</li>
        <li>Linux x64 with glibc or musl</li>
        <li>Windows x64</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        On any other platform, running the CLI fails with an unsupported-platform error. Scan one
        <code>tsconfig.json</code> at a time. In a monorepo, repeat the command for each package
        you want to analyze.
      </p>
      <Callout>
        Dry-run and apply look for <code>node_modules/.bin/tsc</code> in the project, then
        <code>tsc</code> on <code>PATH</code>. Install the project's TypeScript dependency before
        you preview or keep a move.
      </Callout>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <Heading>Install the npm package</Heading>
      <p>
        Published releases are on <TextLink href={NPM_PACKAGE_URL} external>npm</TextLink> and
        <TextLink href={RELEASES_URL} external>GitHub Releases</TextLink>. Source and issues live in
        the <TextLink href={GITHUB_URL} external>repository</TextLink>.
      </p>
      <CodeTabs commands={installCommands} label="Install type-tune" />
      <p>Run the binary that the local install provides:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={`pnpm exec type-tune --version
# after npm install -D type-tune:
npx type-tune --version`} />
      <p>
        <code>pnpm exec</code> and <code>npx</code> use the project install when
        <code>type-tune</code> is already a dependency. A bare
        <code>npx type-tune scan --project tsconfig.json</code> without that dependency downloads
        the package for one invocation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <Heading>Run the first scan</Heading>
      <p>
        <code>scan</code> is read-only. It lists exported <code>type</code> aliases and
        <code>interface</code> declarations in <code>.ts</code> and <code>.tsx</code> files selected
        by the project config. The default <code>--project</code> is <code>tsconfig.json</code>, and
        a candidate needs two referrer files outside its own file.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock code={`pnpm exec type-tune scan --project tsconfig.json
pnpm exec type-tune scan --project tsconfig.json --explain`} />
      <p>
        <code>--explain</code> reports why discovered exported declarations were excluded, such as
        insufficient referrers or an existing type location. Generated files are excluded during
        discovery unless <code>--include-generated</code> is specified. Declaration files such as
        <code>.d.ts</code> are not candidates. Supported <code>.vue</code> script blocks can add
        referrers, and a reviewed move can update their import specifiers. Template and style
        content is left unchanged. Imports that type-tune cannot update safely are rejected. An
        unresolved <code>.vue</code> file, such as a plain <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block, is
        reported by both <code>scan</code> and <code>scan --explain</code> and blocks every move
        from that scan. <code>.svelte</code>, <code>.astro</code>, and JavaScript files are not
        scanned or rewritten.
      </p>
      <p>
        The examples on this site use <code>pnpm exec</code>. After
        <code>npm install -D type-tune</code>, replace that prefix with <code>npx</code> and keep
        the same arguments.
      </p>
      <p>
        When the scan shows a declaration you might move, continue with the
        <TextLink href="/usage/workflow">scan, plan, review, dry-run, and apply workflow</TextLink>.
        To move one symbol you already chose, see the
        <TextLink href="/usage/usecases">extract example</TextLink>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</SiteShell>
